{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493307-gehenna-cults-of-elysium","id":1493307,"title":"Gehenna Cults of Elysium","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-09-18T23:29:37-07:00","id":1493307,"title":"Gehenna Cults of Elysium","content":"I think the Elysium (1994) supplement was one of the stronger sourcebooks for Vampire. I particularly liked its Appendix on Gehenna cults. It has many useful things. But like a lot of lore in VtM, it can be hard to translate what is written into something actually useful at the gaming table. So I will be taking each of the Gehenna cults mentioned in Elysium and trying to figure out how to make good use of them at the game table, and encourage everyone's comments.<br /> <br /> I am assuming people have actual knowledge of the description in Elysium of each of these cults, but understand there may be many people on this forum who don't have access to the books.<br /> <br /> First up is the <b>Royal Order of Edenic Groundskeeper</b> and the <b>Imperial Order of the Master Edenic Groundskeepers</b>. These cults present themselves as a social club for elders. The Royal Order is a Gehenna cult that views the antedeluvians as threats and seeks to gain information about them so they can destroy them. The Imperial Order is a more aggressive version of the cult that advocates action before the actual Gehenna.<br /> <br /> It's a fairly strong concept that has trouble only in figuring out implementing it in the game setting. What we have is a secret conspiracy in a more general club setting, similar to Marvel Comics' Hellfire Club with its Inner Circle. Building on what is mentioned in Elysium, these elders mostly meet at elysiums and have carefully coded conversations, likely withdrawing in private when they need to be more specific about the specific aims of the organization - information about the supposed resting sites of the Antedeluvians in torpor, and the Blood Bound ghouls and vampires they use to patrol them.<br /> <br /> So first we need an Elders only &quot;social club&quot; at elysiums, likely simply called the Edenic Groundskeepers. Or perhaps just &quot;Eden&quot;. Membership should be exclusive - only actual elders, and perhaps limited to those of Status of 3+. In fact, membership to Eden is probably worth an increase in Status by itself. This would just be the cover story. If you qualify, you can likely be invited for membership. But as a member, you may or may not be invited to join the &quot;Royal Order&quot; which is the Gehenna cult. Likewise some members of the &quot;Royal Order&quot; want more aggressive action, and this faction is the &quot;Imperial Order&quot;. They aren't actually separate Gehenna cults, but different factions within the same cult. Nevertheless, because of their size and numbers, the book treats them as separate.<br /> <br /> This is all well and good, but it's a bit of a problem when introducing it within the game because none of the other sourcebooks make use of it.<br /> <br /> I imagine &quot;Eden&quot; holds special member-only events at elysiums around the world. These events are different from the usual meets at an elysium. Most members are not members of the Gehenna cult. But there is a secret &quot;Royal Order&quot; of members who make up the Gehenna cult. They don't &quot;run&quot; Eden as a whole. They just use it as a cover.<br /> <br /> I imagine Europe and the Near East to be the place where most members belong, but North America would certainly have many members.<br /> <br /> Let's use Chicago as an example. (Other cities in North America might not even qualify to have members.) As the de facto &quot;capital&quot; of the Camarilla in North America, Eden should be present. Who would make up the potential members of &quot;Eden&quot; and possibly the &quot;Royal Order&quot;?<br /> <br /> Chicago has a lot of real elders who might qualify. Critias, Tyler, Inyanga, Khalid, Annabelle (and her sire Maria before her), Nicolai, Tyrus, possibly Horace Turnbull, and maybe Sir Henry Johnson. Ironically all the Ventrue of Chicago are far too young, as are many of the &quot;elders&quot; mentioned in the coterie section. They could be candidates in the future once they have aged enough. These are the potential members of the social club &quot;Eden&quot; who hold special events only for themselves, perhaps when hosting visiting elders from Europe and the Old World.<br /> <br /> Who might be actual members of the &quot;Royal Order&quot; and &quot;Imperial Order&quot; of the Gehenna cult? This is much harder to decide as CbN was not written with this in mind. But we can make decisions based on the information about each character.<br /> <br /> Critias, despite being a pawn of Menele, is said to not even suspect the two Methusalehs are in Chicago. He is likely not a member. Tyler is a former member of the Sabbat, whose anti-antedeluvian stance puts it aligned with the Edenic Groundskeepers. Inyanga is harder to place, but her high interest in occult members and general wisdom leads me to place her there. Tyrus also is hard to place, but seems less sophisticated so I'd place him as not. Khalid seems like an obvious member to me. Annabelle isn't, but Nicolai would be. Horace is generally paranoid so much I'm inclined to include him. Sir Henry Johnson probably isn't there yet. So I'd limit the Royal/Imperial Members to be only Tyler, Inyanga, Khalid, Nicolai, and Horace. With Tyler being the most likely member of the Imperial Order.<br /> <br /> In terms of the impact on game, the obvious change is that there's between a half dozen to a full dozen of Chicago elders who are members of &quot;Eden&quot;, a long established social club of true elders - an extended coterie within the Camarilla that run high social vampire galas at elysiums around the world. Membership is restricted, but comes with an increase in Status. And then within it, a very select number of members who belong to an actual Gehenna cult that could provide lots of information that the ST could provide any PCs who manage to infiltrate it.<br /> <br /> I'll provide more posts on other Gehenna cults after enough time for people to comment (if they have any interest to do so).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 10-06-2022, 02:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-19T04:51:49-07:00","id":1493319,"title":"","content":"I follow with interest this thread. That Elysium section is magnificent in its scope but I also struggle to understand how to really use it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26404-submariner93","name":"Submariner93","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26404_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"24"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-19T10:42:33-07:00","id":1493342,"title":"","content":"I was recently re reading that section and wondering how to include it in my CbN game, so this thread is gold for me. I fully agree on Horace being a member, not sold on everyone else yet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27429-microcuchon","name":"Microcuchon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"201"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-19T16:45:01-07:00","id":1493367,"title":"","content":"There's definitely lots of options for which characters should belong. I probably would not argue against any particular line up. So much depends on personal interpretation of each character, personal taste, and the needs of your chronicle. There's a strong case to be made that maybe only Khalid and Horace should be members of the Royal Order if you want to restrict its membership.<br /> <br /> I also forgot to consider the Kindred of Gary. Lucian and Danov would also make good members of &quot;Eden&quot;. Lucian isn't the social type, but since he is able to safely move many vampires across the Atlantic to Chicago and has loads of status, it's an easy justification. Like the other potential members in Chicago By Night, it's much less clear whether they should be members of one of the secret orders.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-19T17:13:57-07:00","id":1493368,"title":"","content":"Related to the social club of &quot;Eden&quot;, an ST may want to populate in his games other types of Kindred social clubs so that Eden does not stick out too much. Local traditions like an annual &quot;Harpies Ball&quot; plus other exclusive or prestigious groups (some of which the PCs may qualify for) would make Eden less conspicuous, even if it is the most prestigious. <br /> <br /> I've done such things in my own chronicles for the purpose of having an interesting background while I'm running some other plot, or a way to allow the PCs to make contacts with the greater Camarilla world beyond the local city. It also helps to just diversify events held at a domain's elysium and provide more interesting flavor to the PCs.<br /> <br /> I've created groupings like the former Archons of the destroyed Justicar Petrodon holding a reunion, parties held every decade (location rotating around the world) for the Golden Circle (a group of vampires who were responsible for moving many vampires around the world during the Age of Discovery and Age of Sail), and the White Rose (an organization of Camarilla vampires known for their exploits against the Sabbat, particularly during the first Camarilla-Sabbat Wars of the Early Modern Period, but including others who fought the Sabbat much later). Some of these have membership requirements so exclusive nobody else can join (Petrodon isn't around anymore to create more Archons), some lax enough that the PCs could join in the short term, and other groups individual PCs might aspire to.<br /> <br /> While not directly related to Gehenna cults, I do think having multiple such social clubs makes a concept like Eden more feasible. And having someone who is a member of Eden and another group would allow PCs to interact with a member of Eden and pick things up without actually being invited. Which is important if your chronicle isn't about Elder PCs (like the Elysium book is about), but your more typical neonate PCs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-20T23:03:03-07:00","id":1493455,"title":"","content":"The next Gehenna cult mentioned is the <b>Way of the Ancient Lawgivers</b>, also known as the Lawgivers or Enochians. Founded in 1312, these elders believe all Kindred must return to the values of the First City and serve the Antedeluvians. By doing so they will delay or even cancel Gehenna. Gehenna is simply a punishment by the Antedeluvians towards their recalcitrant descendants. If you obey the Antedeluvians by acting like vampires did in the First City, then you are safe from Gehenna. The Antedeluvians collectively serve Caine, so by serving the Antedeluvians well its members also serve Caine and make everyone a happy family.<br /> <br /> It seems to basically disavow the Jyhad since the Antedeluvians as a group happily serve Caine and work together. There is no sense that pleasing one Antedeluvian might anger another. The pitfalls of this approach seem clear. But an ST could provide some flavor text to justify the belief.<br /> <br /> Some members simply believe the cult works well in keeping in the younger Kindred in line, but are not set in actually following the Antedeluvians, even if they exist. A secret cell within the Lawgivers believe vampires should dispense with the Masquerade and rule humanity outright.<br /> <br /> I have several comments about this group.<br /> <br /> First, I would make them a front for the Tal'Mahe'Ra, The True Black Hand. In their description, it states they do not accept any Brujah members unless the clan itself forgoes rebellion. This is very close to the True Black Hand;'s refusal to allow any Brujah. I would not say that the True Black Hand controls the Ancient Lawgivers. Just that it has some of its Camarilla members be members of this group, and that they use it as a recruiting pool. I would be heavily tempted to follow the membership requirements of the True Black Hand, meaning the Tremere would also be unlikely members.<br /> <br /> Second, I would encourage STs to actually read and use the Book of Nod (the actual White Wolf sourcebook) to run this group. If an organization is based on running vampire society according to the ideals of the First City, you need to know what that means. The cult description mentions that certain members violate the principles of &quot;the Rules of the Cainalle&quot;. I then looked up the actual Book of Nod sourcebook and saw this is actually a section of the Book of Nod. It's part of the &quot;Chronicles of Shadow&quot; which describes how the First City was run. To understand what the Ancient Lawgivers want to do, understanding this section of the Book of Nod is essential.<br /> <br /> Of course, not all members of the Way of Ancient Lawgivers actually care about that. Many just want to perpetuate elder rule, and the deference to the Antedeluvians - and therefore the elders as their proxies - is part of this. And for some members, that is more important than actually following the precepts of the Book of Nod in regards to the responsibility of elder vampires towards their childer and humanity. So not all members would fit with the True Black Hand. Many members, in fact, would not. But some members would fit, so I think it would be a good recruiting organization for that sect.<br /> <br /> Third, that this cult was founded in 1312, before the Anarch Revolt, is important. Since it predates the Anarchs and Camarilla, it makes the cult very old.<br /> <br /> In terms of how I would use this in the game, I would use them only if I wanted to introduce aspects or plots associated with the True Black Hand. If I wanted to ignore that sect, I would not use this group. I might mention them as some kind of rumor, but not actually introduce in game.<br /> <br /> Using Chicago By Night as an example, I am not sure if any character would be a member. The characters in CbN aren't particularly interested in the Jyhad or Gehenna. There is a partial example of Hind's Socialists where in the coterie example it is mentioned that this coterie has become interested in Gehenna and trying to survive it. But obviously pleasing the Antedeluvians would not be a goal of a coterie of Anarchs. At most, I might make Modius a member since he occupies an odd place as someone who is occult oriented, a Prince, an Anarch, and generally hard to place in ordinary vampire politics. But through him, PCs might find other members.<br /> <br /> I'd have to review other By Night books to find characters who are interested in Gehenna, but whose personality suggests someone who would try to please the Antedeluvians. There might be some, though no one comes to mind.<br /> <br /> While Elysium is based on PCs being elders, and any membership in Gehenna cults based on that status, I do think that unlike the Edenic Groundskeepers (an explicit social club for elders), the non-elders could very well become members. Unlikely, but possibly so. Certainly many elders join the cult on the basis that the First City is a society based on deference to those older than you, there is also a reciprocal obligation - treat those younger than you well. So while neonates and ancillae might be minority members, it is not inconceivable.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":".  Added some sentences for clarification 09-21-2022, 11:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-21T22:52:48-07:00","id":1493507,"title":"","content":"The next Gehenna cult is the <b>Servitors of Irad</b>. At first glance, this cult seems similar to the Ways of the Ancient Lawgivers, but I think there's some important differences. The Servitors of Irad were founded in 1456 during the Anarch Revolt. It does not actively recruit new members. Instead its members work secretly to serve the Antedeluvians and perform the tasks they Antedeluvians want them to do. Unfortunately, they must only guess as what this will be. But they nevertheless seek to be tools of the Jyhad. By being useful, they ensure they will survive when everyone else dies. They kill other elders and try to divide them so that they will be easier pickings during Gehenna. Membership in this cult is illegal and members will be executed by the Archons.<br /> <br /> I know this group is mentioned later in Cults of the Blood Gods, but the little I have read of their description in that book does not impress me. I'm going to ignore it since it was published way after Elysium for my discussion of it. And I think we can do a much better job of using this in games then the way that later book handled them.<br /> <br /> The easiest thing to do is to somehow tie them also into the Tal'Mahe'Ra, but I won't go down that route. The point of having multiple Gehenna cults is to explore different ideas in the setting. Why repeat yourself? I think the Tal'Mahe'Ra may observe this group, maybe even try to manipulate them, but I think they'd try to steer their members away from belonging to it since it is too obvious and too likely to blow the cover of any of their operatives. I want them to be distinct and add something unique to the game, not be duplicative.<br /> <br /> The key to me is the year this group was founded - 1456. This is in the middle of the Anarch Revolt. Several Antedeluvians have been diablerized in the recent past - Lasombra (1405), Tzimisce (1413), and Cappadocious (1444). And it is not that far from when Saulot fell (1133). The Camarilla has just been established, but we're still several decades from its victory culminating in the Treaty of Thorns. I don't think this timing should be considered irrelevant or a coincidence. I think it should be important in figuring out who the Servitors are and what they are trying to do.<br /> <br /> What would a group dedicated to doing the will of the Antedeluvians be doing during this age? While their behavior in modern nights is to disrupt the elders to make them easier to defeat by the Antedeluvians, it seems to me in 1456 their goal would be to avenge the destruction of those Antedeluvians destroyed by the Anarchs, and to prevent more Antedeluvians from being destroyed. Only afterwards would the group begin to look for further goals and other services they could provide. Members might even think that the (some?) diableries actually failed - a trap to see who among their children are loyal or false.<br /> <br /> What about the idea that the antedeluvians are fighting amongst themselves, and thus would not collaborate? There's several ways the Servitors of Irad might answer this objection. One would be that the spate of diablerie during the Anarch Revolt caused them to change plans. They now work together to prevent themselves from being destroyed. Another option might be that the stories of the Jyhad was a lie designed to turn their children against each other so they wouldn't work together and revel against them. Or that their differences will be resolved amicably once Gehenna happens. I'm sure there are more theories others on this forum could add.<br /> <br /> Initially I see its members being from groups most wanting to avenge the destruction of their Antedeluvian. Probably some elder Lasombra (antitribu) that had survived the purge. I would want to add the Old Clan Tzimisce (or if you prefer, just elder Tzimisce that did not join the Sabbat), but I have the feeling they'd be fewer than the Lasombra because of their territoriality and less likely to want to work with others. And there might be some Cappadocians who were more martial supporters of their founder and less prone to pacifism or passiveness, not caring that their founder seemingly wanted to perish. In modern nights, the last two have dwindled, but the Lasombra anti-tribu should make up a sizable number of the modern Servitors. <br /> <br /> At one point, there might even have been some of the last warrior Salubri as members. And one thing we might do is that one of the goals of the Servitors would be too protect any remaining Salubri, as that would be what Saulot (and the other Antedeluvians) would want. I like the irony of making the Servitors of Irad, a group that seems mad and dangerous and an obvious antagonist in the setting, as one of the main protectors of the &quot;soul-sucking&quot; and diablerist Salubri that the NPCs of the setting mainly hate, but whom PCs often like.<br /> <br /> What about the other clans?<br /> <br /> Both Ventrue and Brujah already have destroyed founders, and are proud of their independence. The Brujah because they are rebels, and the Ventrue because of the prestige that their clan is considered to be the least manipulated by the Antedeluvians as a result and thus trusted to provide leadership. I don't see many members of these clans in the Servitors except for those who are particularly perverse or paranoid. After all, they don't have an antedeluvian who will look after them, so they'd be relying on some other clan's founder to protect them which doesn't seem too smart. Likewise, Tremere would also be a bad fit. Any who would join would have to be a strange bird. Not impossible that there are members of these three clans, but not the most likely suspects.<br /> <br /> That leaves the Gangrel, Malkavian, Toreador, and Nosferatu.<br /> <br /> Nosferatu may seem like a strange choice since they believe their Antedeluvian is already after them. But that's exactly why you might have some Nosferatu who think they will be spared by doing the bidding of their Founder. If so, some rumors and legends of the Nictuku might actually be in reference to actions done by Nosferatu members of the Servitors of Irad.<br /> <br /> The Gangrel, Malkavian, and Toreador would all seemingly have real motives to obey their founder. I would think members of these clans would make up the bulk of the Servitors of the Irad outside the Lasombra anti-tribu. I think the Malkavian members might even be key since it is possible some would think they might actually have some communication with their founder given the Madness Network. An elder Malkavian who claims to be given some kind of possible guidance would be someone the other Servitors would listen or even follow. Why not make a Malkavian the leader or one of the leaders of the group? (Assuming of course, you don't make Montano himself the leader of the Servitors as he is the most loyal childe of Lasombra himself. He might have even been the one who founded the group).<br /> <br /> I'm undecided how to approach the Followers of Set and Ravnos in regards to the Servitors. Those clans are fairly distant from the elder elysium culture depicted in Elysium, and estranged from greater vampire society in general. I doubt there are any of their clan members among the Servitors. But it is an intriguing question as to how the Servitors service of the Antedeluvians see those clans, especially the Setites in regards to their own founder. Are they considered enemies? Allies? Potential allies who have gotten things wrong?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-21T22:53:18-07:00","id":1493508,"title":"","content":"It is said the Servitors do not actively recruit. So many of its members are probably very old, and some even date to the original founding. Not actively recruiting is not the same as never recruiting. If they see someone who desires the same things as them, they probably make contact somehow and put her through various tests or verifications. But they are probably a conspiracy of some very powerful elders who have great powers and lots of influence.<br /> <br /> I see the Servitors working as a kind of star chamber within the Camarilla and greater vampire society, waging their own Jyhad based on what they think the Antedeluvians want them to do. Which elders are to be slain? Which plots by the Inner Circle and Justicars are to be thwarted? Whom among the Anarchs will they destroy? How will they gain revenge on the Sabbat and destroy certain key leaders there (like Gratiano, Lambach, and others who participated in the attacks against the Antedeluvians)? Which ploys of the Tremere and Giovanni will they expose?<br /> <br /> One important thing to remember is that they just don't go after Anarchs and enemies of the Camarilla. They want to disrupt the other elders as well because dividing the elders prevents them from uniting against the Antedeluvians. They might even see the Ventrue as one of their greatest enemies because of their supposed independence from the machinations of the Antedeluvians.<br /> <br /> More than the other Gehenna cults in Elysium, the Servitors have the best shot at being active antagonists in a chronicle. An ST should make the most of them. I would probably elevate them to the same status or level as better known groups like the Children of Osiris or even the Black Hand within the Sabbat. Make their name a known element of the setting along with these other groups. &quot;The Servitors of Irad are the hand of the Antedeluvians, if you cross them be prepared to pay the price.&quot; They would also make a great bogeymen for the Sabbat. Whenever they have an unexpected defeat, they might blame the Servitors.<br /> <br /> I'd make all members of the Servitors make oaths similar to the Superman Revenge Squad of DC Comics, but in line to serving the Antedeluvians and destroying their enemies. (Only the first minute on this clip, I could not find a shorter one.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=nYLJANywjkI\" data-code=\"nYLJANywjkI\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/nYLJANywjkI/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> They are not interested in recreating the First City. They may even think it is irrelevant in discussions about Gehenna. But they are loyal to their clan founders (whether out of filial piety, fear, or some other motivation) individually and collectively, and want to survive Gehenna and think they will be rewarded for their faithful service.<br /> <br /> I wouldn't play them as buffoons or thugs. I'd play them as being very dangerous, and more likely to act through proxies and unwitting dupes than do things themselves. But they do dirty their hands when needed - when their personal power is necessary to see a task done. But secrecy is still very important to them. When things go wrong in a domain, when important Kindred mysteriously die, etc., they are one of the groups named as perhaps being responsible along with the other usual suspects. And of course, they act in other disruptive ways that do not deal in violence at all - sabotaging votes at Conclaves, spreading false rumors, and so on.<br /> <br /> Instead of being antagonists to the PCs, they might make interesting patrons who use the PCs as unwitting pawns. That elder who helps the Anarch PC against the Prince really isn't sympathetic to the Anarchs. He just wants to weaken the Prince before the next Conclave is held so he won't rally the votes needed to pass an important resolution. Or because he wants the Prince dead as he's too effective in uniting the other elders together. Or he helps a PC get revenge on someone the Servitors have already targeted for destruction. It's just really good the blame will go to the PC instead of the elder.<br /> <br /> I can't see any NPCs in Chicago By Night (or even other cities) being members of the Servitors. None really have the right mentality. We might be more successful by looking at Dark Ages sourcebooks and picking a character who would be a good member. As I said, I think Montano might be a good choice.<br /> <br /> I do see characters like Son and Nathaniel Bordruff as being of possible use to the Servitors of Irad. But nothing in their background would indicate a propensity to join. Gehenna just doesn't seem a factor to them. But I would be willing to create an NPC in Chicago who is a member, who would try to make use of both of these characters when necessary.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":".  typos 09-22-2022, 07:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-22T04:24:49-07:00","id":1493518,"title":"","content":"I am running a 1991 Chicago/USA Chronicle and your post inspired me to make Xaviar a Servitor of Irad","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27429-microcuchon","name":"Microcuchon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"201"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-28T16:13:22-07:00","id":1493937,"title":"","content":"Every ST will come up with a different list of goals for the Servitors of Irad. Some goals and very specific, and others very vague. The vague ones would lead to any number of lesser plots. It is not important whether any of these goals are actually the will of one or more Antedeluvians. It's just that it would make sense for the Servitors of Irad to believe their goals reasonably reflect those wills. In fact, I like the idea that in certain goals the Servitors are likely helping the Antedeluvians, that others do nothing, and some are even against their wishes.<br /> <br /> But I do think if one is going to use the Servitors of Irad, they'd work best if they had specific goals. I must admit I have never used them (or any other Gehenna cult) in game. But now that I've given them some thought, the below would be the goals I'd consider using for them.<ul><li>Hunt down and destroy Sabbat Archbishop Gratiano (of Rio de Janeiro I believe, though I don't know when this city was mentioned), the diablerist (or would-be diablerist) of Lasombra. Slay any of his surviving conspirators among the Lasombra and Assamites who were with him.</li> <li>Find the secret torpor ground of the diablerist (or would be diablerist) of Tzimisce, Lugoj the Bloodbreaker, and destroy him. Slay his surviving conspirators like Lambach. (We know Lugoj is actually dead, but to everyone else - other than Lambach - in the setting, Lugoj succeeded but simply disappeared and put himself in torpor until he will rise again. So I think discovering his hiding place so they can destroy him would be a goal. It's not the Servitors' fault that their primary target is already destroyed.)</li> <li>Hunt down and destroy Augustus Giovanni, Claudius Giovanni, and the surviving members of the Conspiracy of Isaac - those who destroyed Cappadocious.</li> <li>Discover a way to ruin the Vaulderie and restore the ability of the blood bond.</li> <li>Protect the surviving Salubri.</li> <li>Protect the surviving true Cappadocians.</li> <li>Undermine the Tremere Inner Council of Seven and thwart their plans.</li> <li>Undermine the Giovanni and thwart their plans.</li> <li>Prevent the Camarilla's Inner Circle, Justicars, and Archons from increasing their power and authority so that the elders are kept divided and weak.</li> <li>Identify any elders actively working against the Antedeluvians or trying to stop Gehenna so they can be destroyed.</li> <li>Aid the Nictuku by destroying select elders of the Nosferatu who can organize the others to oppose them.</li> <li>Find and destroy the Ravnos Methusaleh Vestgeir who saved the Sabbat.</li> <li>Thwart the Sabbat and destroy the Black Hand.</li> <li>Punish and destroy all diablerists when able.</li> <li>Suppress knowledge of the Thaumaturgical Ritual of the Bitter Rose.</li> <li>Confirm the destruction of Troile at Carthage and if she was not, find her and destroy her.</li> <li>Aid in defeating the modern Anarchs.</li> <li>Overthrow the Anarch Free State in Los Angeles.</li> <li>Learn the secrets of the Inconnu and determine if they are a threat to the Antedeluvians and their plans.</li> <li>Discover the resting locations of the Antedeluvians and protect them from their enemies.</li> <li>Infiltrate and destroy from within other Gehenna cults.</li> </ul>This list is not meant to be comprehensive. I'm sure there are other good ideas that do not even occur to me. Nor do I think the Servitors need to be acting on so many goals. The actual things they do may be more limited.<br /> <br /> Even this list reveals A LOT of goals that the Servitors likely have. Many of them are probably impossible to achieve, but I don't think the Servitors are actually that concerned about fulfilling each one. It is more important that demonstrate sincerity in carrying out the desires of the Antedeluvians. The Servitors may not even coordinate as a group. It might be individual Kindred acting and asking support when needed. Or local cells cooperating. Or a mix of different levels of cooperation depending on the mission. And of course, being a member of the Servitors of Irad is not a full time job. Different members will have different levels of commitment, time, and effort that they spend.<br /> <br /> They may even think their contributions are meaningless since nothing can prevent Gehenna from happening and the Antedeluvians from winning. They don't even necessarily need to believe that Lasombra is actually destroyed for example. Destroying Gratiano doesn't need to be done out of avenging Lasombra, but simply because a hidden Lasombra would want such temerity punished and reward anyone who did after he returns.<br /> <br /> The other thing any list does is provide lots of opportunities for the Servitors to have succeeded, and present it as lore or legend in the setting. It might be interesting in a Sabbat game for some NPCs to speculate why some of the Anarchs that accompanied Gratiano to Lasombra's tomb have either been destroyed or mysteriously disappeared every few decades. Is someone trying to kill them? Or to explain some of the aid rendered to Salubri or Cappadocians over the years - the PCs might think that some elder is really just being a nice person, when in fact it is done simply out of spite against two clans the Servitors consider to be foes. Or to have the Servitors prioritize one particular diablerist as an enemy and make sure she is hounded until destroyed. That lets the STs to sprinkle all kind of rumors that the Servitors are responsible for this event or that one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 09-28-2022, 07:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-28T16:51:10-07:00","id":1493938,"title":"","content":"The <b>Twilight Cult</b> was founded in 1550 by Ventrue and Tremere elders although I am assuming there are now members of many clans. Members of this cult believe one stanza in the Book of Nod referencing \u201cthe last daughter of Eve\u201d is the key to preventing Gehenna. They believe this woman will bear the \u201cmark of Eve\u201d - a crescent moon. But some believe this is some kind of psychic powers, while others think it is a Numina, a pattern of her aura, a birthmark, or something else. The cult's members seem divided whether they should be attempting to prevent the birth of this woman, or to attempt to control her.<br /> <br /> We also know the cult exists in Paris, but presumably it can have members in many other cities, and they might hold meetings elsewhere. But simply on the basis of that city's mention, I'd be tempted to make Paris where the senior members of the cult meet. At least for those members in Europe. North American members might meet in a closer city for them, and only travel to Paris rarely.<br /> <br /> A stranger aspect of this cult is their belief that the last daughter of Eve will be born among the Gypsies, and this involves them in a lot of intrigues involving that group. Early edition VtM really believed in using the classic Gypsy horror movie trope in its setting. I think it's OK using that in a limited fashion, but it is very heavy handed. While close reading of the game actually reveals that the supernatural elements among the Roma is separated from the ethnic group as a whole, it is very easy to miss that and assume the game is talking about the entire ethnic group. And this section of the Gehenna Cults appendix in Elysium clearly falls into that latter category. However, I believe this entire section involving the Roma could easily be transferred to any other kind of group, including non-ethnic ones, or be abandoned entirely. I think the only real benefit of limiting it to a single group (however defined) is that it makes the haystack much, much smaller; and therefore the Twilight Cult becomes easier to use in the game.<br /> <br /> Like the mention of the Book of Nod in the Twilight Cult, I went back to my copy of the Book of Nod published by White Wolf to see if the &quot;last daughter of Eve&quot; was actually mentioned. She is! She is mentioned in the section called the Book of Secrets along with the other prophecies of Gehenna. Prior to doing this, I had never really bothered to read the Book of Nod. I thought it would be merely fluff and lots of flavor without anything being actually gameable. But it seems this sourcebook is probably essential if an ST wanted to include many of the Gehenna Cults in Elysium. The ST will want to familiarize herself with the relevant sections, and determine what clues or excerpts of the Book will be given to the PCs.<br /> <br /> I see only two ways an ST can really use this cult. The first is a plot that deals with the cult attempting to find this last daughter of Eve (though I don't think it is actually important for their target to actually BE the last daughter of Eve), and all the complications that ensue. This would probably be one of the major plots of any chronicle. The second involves using this cult as a means to increase interaction with one of the other supernatural factions that may somehow be involved in this person as whole. Both could be part of a story leading up to Gehenna in a chronicle (if this woman is actually found), or just as a red herring because the woman is not yet born and anyone the cult is hunting for is just someone else.<br /> <br /> The hard part in using this cult would be to figuring out how exactly the last daughter of Eve is important to Gehenna, and how the cultists think they can use her. Even if she doesn't come up in game, the ST will need to come up with various theories by any cult members on this person. In any case, involving this cult will require a lot of Noddism introduced in the game.<br /> <br /> I think the Twilight Cult is an interesting flavor text, but not heavily gameable. I do wonder what Andrew Greenberg intended for this cult, as the cross references were obviously done on purpose. was it just a Mystery Box style BS that would be totally left to the STs? Or was something more intended where the puzzle pieces scattered through a variety of sourcebooks were to be put together?<br /> <br /> Furthermore, was the last daughter of Eve ever mentioned in any future sourcebooks? I have never read the Gehenna book so I am curious as to whether she is ever referenced there.<br /> <br /> Because the cult is so specific, I don't think it's possible to identify potential members in any sourcebook. If I absolutely had to use the Twilight Cult in Chicago, I'd probably pick Modius, Nicolai, and Horace Turnbull as members simply to have some variety in members that kind of make sense. Maybe with one or two more members pulled from Gary and Milwaukee. It's be interesting to see someone like Parovich show up. But there's nothing in these characters backgrounds that would make them obvious members. It would probably be best if the ST created their own characters to be members, or to partially rewrite the backgrounds of any canonical characters she would want to make members. Potential members who already have an interest in the occult or the Jyhad would make the most sense.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 10-06-2022, 02:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https:/