is cult is their belief that the last daughter of Eve will be born among the Gypsies, and this involves them in a lot of intrigues involving that group. Early edition VtM really believed in using the classic Gypsy horror movie trope in its setting. I think it's OK using that in a limited fashion, but it is very heavy handed. While close reading of the game actually reveals that the supernatural elements among the Roma is separated from the ethnic group as a whole, it is very easy to miss that and assume the game is talking about the entire ethnic group. And this section of the Gehenna Cults appendix in Elysium clearly falls into that latter category. However, I believe this entire section involving the Roma could easily be transferred to any other kind of group, including non-ethnic ones, or be abandoned entirely. I think the only real benefit of limiting it to a single group (however defined) is that it makes the haystack much, much smaller; and therefore the Twilight Cult becomes easier to use in the game.<br /> <br /> Like the mention of the Book of Nod in the Twilight Cult, I went back to my copy of the Book of Nod published by White Wolf to see if the &amp;quot;last daughter of Eve&amp;quot; was actually mentioned. She is! She is mentioned in the section called the Book of Secrets along with the other prophecies of Gehenna. Prior to doing this, I had never really bothered to read the Book of Nod. I thought it would be merely fluff and lots of flavor without anything being actually gameable. But it seems this sourcebook is probably essential if an ST wanted to include many of the Gehenna Cults in Elysium. The ST will want to familiarize herself with the relevant sections, and determine what clues or excerpts of the Book will be given to the PCs.<br /> <br /> I see only two ways an ST can really use this cult. The first is a plot that deals with the cult attempting to find this last daughter of Eve (though I don't think it is actually important for their target to actually BE the last daughter of Eve), and all the complications that ensue. This would probably be one of the major plots of any chronicle. The second involves using this cult as a means to increase interaction with one of the other supernatural factions that may somehow be involved in this person as whole. Both could be part of a story leading up to Gehenna in a chronicle (if this woman is actually found), or just as a red herring because the woman is not yet born and anyone the cult is hunting for is just someone else.<br /> <br /> The hard part in using this cult would be to figuring out how exactly the last daughter of Eve is important to Gehenna, and how the cultists think they can use her. Even if she doesn't come up in game, the ST will need to come up with various theories by any cult members on this person. In any case, involving this cult will require a lot of Noddism introduced in the game.<br /> <br /> I think the Twilight Cult is an interesting flavor text, but not heavily gameable. I do wonder what Andrew Greenberg intended for this cult, as the cross references were obviously done on purpose. was it just a Mystery Box style BS that would be totally left to the STs? Or was something more intended where the puzzle pieces scattered through a variety of sourcebooks were to be put together?<br /> <br /> Furthermore, was the last daughter of Eve ever mentioned in any future sourcebooks? I have never read the Gehenna book so I am curious as to whether she is ever referenced there.<br /> <br /> Because the cult is so specific, I don't think it's possible to identify potential members in any sourcebook. If I absolutely had to use the Twilight Cult in Chicago, I'd probably pick Modius, Nicolai, and Horace Turnbull as members simply to have some variety in members that kind of make sense. Maybe with one or two more members pulled from Gary and Milwaukee. It's be interesting to see someone like Parovich show up. But there's nothing in these characters backgrounds that would make them obvious members. It would probably be best if the ST created their own characters to be members, or to partially rewrite the backgrounds of any canonical characters she would want to make members. Potential members who already have an interest in the occult or the Jyhad would make the most sense.</div> </div> </div> </div>The last daughter of Eve is one of the main plots in Cairo by Night sourcebook, which is heavily loaded with Gehena stuff (it's actually the book theme and mood). Curiously enough, despite being various groups looking for her, the Twilight Cult are not mentioned at all <br /> And you have sold me on Modius. In my canon he was in Paris until 1871 and he was involved with different groups, so that may be where he learned thaumaturgy","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27429-microcuchon","name":"Microcuchon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"201"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-01T11:27:04-07:00","id":1494104,"title":"","content":"The <b>Ninth Wave</b> is a relatively late Gehenna cult being founded only in 1890. All the others were founded centuries earlier. This cult believes Gehenna will arrive when magic in the world reaches a critical mass, which they term \u201cthe second Flood\u201d. The level of magic is what keeps the Antedeluvians in torpor or makes them active. Therefore, the Ninth Wave acts to destroy and suppress the level of magic in this world in order to prevent the Antedeluvians from waking.<br /> <br /> The cult's name comes from an Alfred, Lord Tennyson poem inside the Idylls of the King. The Idylls is a cycle of twelve narrative poems in blank verse about the Arthurian legends. The Ninth Wave is an excerpt for the first idyll, the Coming of Arthur. The last idyll was published in 1885, only five years before the cult was established. This is likely not a coincidence. For convenience, I am posting an excerpt here.<br /> <br /> <i>Wave after wave, each mightier than the last,</i><br /> <i>Till last, a ninth one, gathering half the deep</i><br /> <i>And full of voices, slowly rose and plunged</i><br /> <i>Roaring, and all the wave was in a flame:</i><br /> <i>And down the wave and in the flame was borne</i><br /> <i>A naked babe, and rode to Merlin's feet,</i><br /> <i>Who stoopt and caught the babe, and cried, 'The King!</i><br /> <i>Here is an heir for Uther!' And the fringe</i><br /> <i>Of that great breaker, sweeping up the strand,</i><br /> <i>Lashed at the wizard as he spake the word,</i><br /> <i>And all at once all round him rose in fire,</i><br /> <i>So that the child and he were clothed in fire.</i><br /> <i>And presently thereafter followed calm,</i><br /> <i>Free sky and stars.</i><br /> <br /> According to this <a href=\"https://caucus99percent.com/content/tuesday-open-thread-ninth-wave\" target=\"_blank\">webpage</a>, the phrase the ninth wave is an old sailing expression. It refers to a monster-like wave following a succession of incrementally larger ones. Depicted in literature as the Wave of Transformation, writers and poets have portrayed this transformation as both startling and serene.<br /> <br /> This leads to all sorts of crossover goodness if you like that kind of thing. It pits the vampires of this cult against any Mages except the Technocrachy. In fact, there is even an alliance between these vampires and the Technocracy of sorts, although it is mainly limited to the vampires providing funding for their labs. But if an ST wanted, this could easily change to something more ignorant and the vampires of the Ninth Wave only inadvertently supporting the Technocracy. But I think it was clearly intended to justify crossover with Mage.<br /> <br /> These vampires also want to exterminate the fairies. They believe that the &quot;return&quot; of the faeries to Earth is a major reason Gehenna will begin. In fact, they call this return &quot;the ninth wave&quot; which could imply that fairy extermination is the major reason for the cult to exist in the first place. Elysium was published in 1994, and first edition Changeling was to be published the next year. I don't think certain specifics had been worked out by then, but I do think this Gehenna cult was written with Changeling in mind using what basic information they had at the time. Vampire-Changeling conflict has never really been a thing in either gameline, but if you want to justify it then the Ninth Wave is for you.<br /> <br /> The Ninth Wave is also obsessed about the Masquerade since revealing that vampires exist would only make people think the supernatural is real, and that would lead to even more magic in the world. As a result, they absolutely hate the Sabbat because of its lack of concern over the Masquerade. Since the Lasombra and Tzimisce did not actually destroy their antedeluvian clan founders, and the Black Hand of the Sabbat was secretly controlled by the Tal'Mahe'Ra, this probably makes the Ninth Wave the most effective Gehenna cult so far. They seem to get a lot of things right, tying it into the greater World of Darkness lines being published, though the specifics of the level of magic and the antedeluvians waking has never been established.<br /> <br /> I don't think it makes sense to use this Gehenna cult in a Vampire game unless the ST intends to use a lot of crossover. If you don't, you lose most of the reason the cult exists and would seem to be pretty boring. But there are some aspects of the cult that might be played.<br /> <br /> First, the cult's understanding of the nature of magical reality is very much based on the hermetic understanding of the Tremere (and through them the Order of Hermes and Mage). Tremere can often make bad members of many Gehenna cults because they don't have ties to an Antedeluvian or the First City except in a roundabout way. But they would be very important members in this cult. Second, its relatively recent founding means this could be a Gehenna cult that is much more open to ancillae and neonates rather than elders. The cult is approaching Gehenna in a very different way than other Gehenna cults, suggesting a great deal of innovation and creativity. Elders are not known for that. I think these approaches should be emphasized to distinguish the Ninth Wave from other Gehenna cults. If you can think of any other indirect ways to prevent the Antedeluvians from waking (they don't involve direct assaults or attacks on their torpored bodies), I would add it to this group. I like the novelty that vampires might prevent Gehenna by doing something that indirectly affects the Antedeluvians.<br /> <br /> The other trivia we are given about the cult is that they maintain an extensive library in Edinburgh, Scotland. The library contains a lot of occult information - magical tomes, ancient manuscripts, vials of Lupine, Faerie, and Mage blood to be used for experimentation, and a &quot;complete map of Earth's ley lines&quot;.<br /> <br /> So Edinburgh is probably the headquarters of the cult. Otherwise why maintain such an expensive library there? Traditionally, Scotland is supposed to be a stronghold of the Toreador. So I would include local Toreador as some prominent members of this cult. I would also create an Edinburgh Toreador that is the clan's foremost expert on Lord Tennyson and the Idylls of the King. After all, she was the one who probably named the cult after the poem.<br /> <br /> Few vampires are able to maintain such an extensive occult library. I would posit then that the local Tremere Chantry at Edinburgh are members of this cult as well, and that this library is maintained by the Tremere (although it may not be an official part of the chantry since non-Tremere members of the cult will want to access it too). Edinburgh is not that big of a city. Metro area is only around 900,000 people. There might be ten vampires there (and we need to fit in some Dunsirn as well - though as an interesting thought maybe a Dunsirn is a member). So maybe the Tremere chantry is only one or two vampires. But if that is the case, it'd be very easy for this Tremere regent to devote his chantry to the needs of a Gehenna cult. To other Tremere, they're just getting a lot of reports about experiments with fairy blood. And who would associate that with membership in a Gehenna cult? Who knows, maybe much of the domain of Edinburgh is part of the Ninth Wave. It'd be interesting flavor for anyone who wants to do an Edinburgh (or Scotland) by Night chronicle.<br /> <br /> Of course, any good Gehenna cult has to be found elsewhere. But if the Ninth Wave was founded in 1890 in Edinburgh, realistically it would spread when vampires who were members in Edinburgh moved to other cities and recruited new members in that city. This would make the cult rather small, and likely concentrated in the British Isles with only a few domains outside that area.<br /> <br /> So we have limited options if we go to Chicago By Night. But there is one possible candidate found in the second edition book - the Malkavian Corbin from &quot;the Trinity&quot;. I must admit that while I do like the character and background of these three characters, that they took up seven whole pages for them (plus one more in the Coteries description). That's 2+ pages per vampire when most vampires in the book were about a page. And their &quot;coterie&quot; was so self-contained that they could have been set anywhere. There is no reason for them to be in Chicago. This was clearly a case of someone's favorite character idea being planted in a city book that didn't need them.<br /> <br /> But for our purposes, Corbin has established ties to Edinburgh and only left that area when other Kindred threatened her and she randomly picked Chicago as her next haven. Her dementia is extreme fixation so that if something catches her interest, she becomes obsessed with it. In other words, it would be really easy to justify her being a member of the Ninth Wave if the ST so wanted. We just need to say that as a Malkavian who spent much of her time in Edinburgh, she was introduced to the cult, thought it interesting, and is now an obsessed member of it. When she had to flee to Chicago, her obsession would continue and she would want to recruit new members to the Ninth Wave so she could continue working on her interest. In fact, if we went down that route, Corbin could easily have been one of its founding members.<br /> <br /> If you wanted to use the Trinity in a Chicago chronicle, but couldn't figure out why any PC would want to enter that mess, then you now have one way to do so. If a PC would be interested in a group like the Ninth Wave, then they could become involved in Corbin through whatever means the ST devises to introduce them. Then that triangle becomes a nice complication to spring AFTER they become an associate of her.<br /> <br /> I would likely not use the Ninth Wave myself unless I really wanted to include crossover, or have come up with other indirect means a cult might &quot;stop&quot; the Antedeluvians. I would not have it be a very large group, and would likely limit its presence to one vampire recently arrived in the domain who had been a member back in the British Isles. But one positive thing about the group is that its members seem reliably informed on a lot of things, and you have justification for travel to Edinburgh to visit the library if you ever want to run that in your chronicle. It would also provide an established, but still small Gehenna cult that the PCs might become important members in, and that would not be possible in most other cults that have many more members and been around much longer. And you can play a lot with an established theme - Tennyson's poem and his other works - as a way for members of the cult to provide secret clues to each other that they need to meet. That kind of flavor text and use in game is something that players tend to love, and it is lacking with some of the other Gehenna cults. This would be a very easy theme for STs and PCs to include.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 10-06-2022, 02:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-03T23:59:52-07:00","id":1494288,"title":"","content":"The last Gehenna cult described in the Elysium sourcebook is the poorly named <b>Cult of Enlightenment</b>. After some very evocative names, I guess a banal one was inevitable. The Cult of Enlightenment was founded in AD 510. Its members believe that Gehenna can be prevented by elevating the general level of \u201cenlightened\u201d thinking in the world. Its original three founders were elders each of the Salubri, Tzimisce, and Nosferatu clans. Achieving Golconda is an important task, but not the only one for its members. The cult has declined in recent centuries as older members disappear, and new members aren't very concerned about the cult's actual purpose.<br /> <br /> The three founding elders have not been seen for more than a thousand years, and its unknown if they are still active, destroyed, or in torpor. The three founders are more than just trivia, since they wrote down of body of knowledge that is the basis of cult doctrine. It includes their conversations with Tibetan monks, aboriginal wise people, Mages, elder Lupines, and so on - the typical shorthand in early WoD for &quot;wisdom&quot;. The number of these &quot;scrolls of enlightenment&quot; are said to be either eight, nine, or thirteen. The cult today only has three of the original scrolls (they may have others as copies). There are various rumors on who have the other original scrolls including the usual suspects like &quot;the Mages&quot;. The Arcanum might also have one.<br /> <br /> Another rumor states that members of the Cult approached the Catholic Church, hoping to learn more wisdom, but it only confirmed that vampires existed and may have been one of the reasons the Inquisition (which I am assuming they mean the Society of Leopold) formed.<br /> <br /> Early cult members were said to be very successful in achieving Golconda, but that has severely declined. Most of its members are very ancient, even for elders, and they are part of the cult in the hope it will help them save their last vestiges of Humanity and avoid the Wassail.<br /> <br /> The weakest aspect of this cult is the weak reasoning behind why a &quot;semi-universal enlightenment&quot; would prevent Gehenna. How exactly was this idea reached? At least the theories of the Ninth Wave are actually plausible in terms of WoD metaphysics even if not proven. But the Gehenna aspect of this Gehenna cult is rather weak.<br /> <br /> Like all the cults, it is also underdeveloped. An ST is going to have to do a lot of work preparing the Cult of Enlightenment for her game. She will need to create the names and backstories of the three founding elders, the basic ideas of their philosophy, lots of flavor text, and description of at least some of these scrolls.<br /> <br /> However, there is a lot this cult potentially offers PCs. First, it can be used to pass information about Golconda. Second, I'm interpreting the oblique mention of &quot;Tibetan monks&quot; means the Children of Osiris, so it is a way to pass on knowledge about this small sect. Probably the Inconnu as well. Third, you could possibly use it to introduce certain crossover factions - say certain Mages and Stargazer tribe of Garou. Third, you can use the &quot;original scrolls of enlightenment&quot; as a MacGuffin hunt to send the PCs around the world trying to find the scrolls, or just one scroll as an important plot point in a single domain. Fourth, you have the mystery of the disappeared three founders. Were they destroyed? If so, by who and when? Can they be found and woken up? Is one or more still active?<br /> <br /> Unlike the other cults, this cult is not about destroying any other vampires. So it is much more useful in generation conversations with NPCs and intrigue. I think lots of things can be done using this cult by an ST.<br /> <br /> The year of its founding might refer to when the original three founders started their quest (before they wrote the original scrolls), or after they had completed writing the scrolls and began sharing their knowledge with others. Simply to give the cult a decent amount of time to be useful, I would say that 510 best represents when the founders wrote down their scrolls.<br /> <br /> The Western Roman Empire had fallen less than fifty years previously, but the Classical World had not yet died. Neither the destructive Gothic Wars of Justinian in Italy, or the Muslim conquests that disrupted the existing Mediterranean trade world had happened yet. But the old Roman order was in seriously decline. I would rule that the previous century's decline had worried the original founders, and they could see a coming civilizational collapse. They might have believed they were in the first stages of Gehenna much like vampires of the game's publishing era was supposed to believe.<br /> <br /> While the three founders had to travel far and wide in their journeys, the cult needs to have originated at a single place. Constantinople probably works best. Then its members would have moved around the rest of the world. Unfortunately, we can't use any of the canon characters in Constantinople By Night since the founders would have disappeared before then. But we can use it to identify characters who might have been members of the Cult of Enlightenment at the time of that sourcebook. They would fit the cult members descriptions as being ancient. I looked for people who were scholarly or spiritual, but not tied directly into the Michael plotline or have some other goal or interest that would be distracting.<br /> <br /> The Brujah scholars of Theophilus and Dorotheus are possibilities given their age, their intelligence, and that they are beginning to read the signs of Gehenna.<br /> <br /> I think it would be interesting if Myca Vykos was once a member, later changing his views about Gehenna and using any knowledge gained about &quot;enlightenment&quot; to help form the Sabbat Paths of Enlightenment.<br /> <br /> Achmet the Dreamer of the Salubri, and leader of the Dream Circle (which also include Myca). Many of the other members of the Dream Circle would also make good members, but I'm reluctant to simply make this coterie and the Cult of Enlightenment one in the same. If having a Salubri was too obvious, I'd switch Achmet out for Gregory the Wonder Maker.<br /> <br /> There are certainly other characters who could fit, and would be interested in hearing which ones people would select.<br /> <br /> Once again, this is a cult that does not easily track to any characters in a more modern city like described in Chicago By Night. But I'd likely make Critias be AWARE of them, though never a member. And he might be able to put a PC into contact with an old acquaintance he knew was or is in the cult. If Critias' Humanity ever started declining or he began developing Methusaleh's Hunger, then he might change his mind about the Cult and attempt to join it in desperate hope it has some secret that could help him exactly as so many others have done.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 10-06-2022, 02:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-13T14:12:39-07:00","id":1494936,"title":"","content":"<b>Summary</b><br /> <br /> Overall, I think there is a lot of good in this Appendix of Elysium. Each of the cults provides a different experience for a PC. Even cults like the Ancient Lawgivers and Servitors of Irad that have a similar purpose, have a very different way of implementing. I like that a lot. We don't have duplicate concepts, but a wide variety. But what does this mean for actual chronicles?<br /> <br /> Like a lot of concepts in the World of Darkness, an easy mistake would be to use them too much. Few cities should have even one such cult represented, and very few would have more than one. There can only be so many cults and so many members.<br /> <br /> While I used Chicago By Night characters as examples of how to use most of the individual cults, I actually would not use every cult there. I would probably introduce the &quot;Eden&quot; social club that meets several times a year for gala celebrations, and have a few members of it be secret members of the Edenic Groundskeepers (instead of presenting rival &quot;Royal Order&quot; and &quot;Imperial Order&quot;, I'd just have the members argue a lot when they met). I might add the Ninth Wave after Corbin arrives into the city. And that would be it. Even a large domain like Chicago can have only so many Gehenna cultists. If I had a specific plot in mind that required one of the other Gehenna cults, I'd just run that.<br /> <br /> For chronicles sent in other domains, I'd want to change it up and have no Gehenna cults at all or use a different one. If the elders were firmly in control, maybe use the Way of the Ancient Lawgivers for example.<br /> <br /> I will probably start using a Camarilla-wide Elysium social clubs like &quot;Eden&quot; (as well as some others) as a general complement to the setting. But I would not place secret members of the Edenic Groundskeeper in every city where the general membership of Eden exists. The general membership of Eden should not know about the Edenic Groundskeepers, though that may be something that is gradually learned about.<br /> <br /> Rather than trying to shoehorn in any of these Gehenna cults in a chronicle and fit NPCs to them, I think it is better to consider groups like these cults as STs create the NPCs for their chronicles. How might Chicago By Night have changed if the concept of these Gehenna cults existed beforehand, and it was intended certain NPCs would belong in them (or perhaps be potential invitees as the chronicle progresses). Thinking about these things beforehand can help diversify NPC concepts and give key characters ties to the greater setting.<br /> <br /> I think mysteries are very important for a game like vampire. And Gehenna cult membership (or sympathies with such a cult) is something that can be easily added. It gives more depths to those characters, gives them something else to do, and can act as a means to tie the PCs to the greater world of Darkness.<br /> <br /> For the most part, I'd restrict membership to elders and ancillae. Neonates just don't have the experience or mentality to truly be members. Their thoughts are not on Gehenna, but building their own power and complaining about domain politics. Though PCs might become involved as pawns or servants of the members of such cults. I would probably also try to keep membership to less than 10% of the total population of elders and ancillae. Gehenna cult membership should be rare, not typical. Fewer is better.<br /> <br /> But what if you want to show off these concepts. Well, I'd expand the game then. Conclaves can be a great use here. Conclaves can last for months as many Kindred gather to debate whatever the issues are. Vampire population of a city swells in size. What a perfect time then for many such Gehenna cults to arrive, and its members find time to secretly meet and discuss business. That could be worth several game sessions of play, and leave some of the PCs with important contacts with vampires in other cities. By enlarging the setting that way, more concepts can be used without it seeming like everyone in the home domain is secretly a member of some cult or other group.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-13T19:57:53-07:00","id":1494955,"title":"","content":"Early Vampire had a lot of fun, provoking and interesting concepts. And I love the Elysium book.<br /> One little problem in WoD is always that it lacks new systems and mechanics to explore and live this other playstyles. Just getting to have more powers and higher traits isn't enough, the true gameplay is still the same, and yeah, it is by no means something easy to implement. But unfortunately it isn't, Only Mind Eyes Theatre did something interesting with Status effects and Backgrounds. But either way, Camarilla feels like the secret Cult heavy sect, having so many elders and all that. Secret societies within secret societies. It was weird how they ended up stating that Cam has this almost stupid banning of Noddism, so furiously that calls way too much attention. And social cults and rituals are so much of a Camarilla thing, mixed with paranoia and dark secrets.<br /> <br /> Elysium really makes it feel like that. And Elder become this alien statuesque, antique monsters that force your cordiality with it, as it forces itself cordial with its peers.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Strange... When coincidence seems too convenient, I prefer to call it fate.</i><br /> <br /> <b><sup>-Blood Omen: Legacy of Kain</sup> </b>d=","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25534-banu_saulot","name":"Banu_Saulot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25534_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-13T23:07:24-07:00","id":1494958,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Banu_Saulot</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493307-gehenna-cults-of-elysium?p=1494955#post1494955\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> But either way, Camarilla feels like the secret Cult heavy sect, having so many elders and all that. Secret societies within secret societies.</div> </div> </div> </div>I feel an important part of the game is what I call &quot;Jyhad level mysteries&quot;, meaning intrigues, mysteries, and plots that are not immediately apparent. I consider organizations like the Tal'Mahe'Ra and Children of Osiris to be part of that. And Gehenna cults fit that as well.<br /> <br /> Because they are organizations, they're better than individual mysteries like the rivalry between Menele and Helena in Chicago By Night. I've never played in a game where any PC ever figured that out (at least without the ST spoon feeding it to us, which defeats the purpose in my opinion). But when there are groups, these concepts become more apparent.<br /> <br /> It is so easy for the game to become mired in politics that only affect a single city/domain. Having these higher level mysteries that transcend city politics creates a sense of wonder and mystery.<br /> <br /> However, it is something that I always struggled with as an ST. It is hard to introduce these different levels of mystery/intrigue. But it is here that concepts like Gehenna cults can really help an ST. They are an overlooked, but critical means that an ST can add an extra &quot;layer&quot; to their vampire game because they make them understandable to the ST. They are no longer vague concepts, but something concrete an ST can start using and develop real, actionable plots.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-14T11:26:22-07:00","id":1494976,"title":"","content":"I feel the same. That is why I think there should be more chronicles that have a semi-open sandbox way of dealing with. A conflict within a domain that can be resolved through different ways by the party, getting to know the factions, power players, structures and cultures of the city. This gives way more space for cults and conspiracies to flourish, cause otherwise they probably will lay low and so PCs wont find them, which is the problem Black Fox has talked about.<br /> <br /> One of my most fun games was the 2nd chapter of Under the Black Cross chronicle book, the one that occurs in Acre. Because the PCs get in the middle of a political conflict of different groups, deal with both Cainites and mortals, see some crazy shit, deal with social intrigue and can resort to violence in some cases but not all. In the end they help decide the fate of the domain. It is a great example of how official chronicles should be written when they attempt to make a political game.<br /> <br /> And one day I will make a chronicle about a cult war in a city, vying for control, even if it means allying with other cults against a bigger one. Anarchs are fertile grounds for multiple cults and Camarilla is an excellent place to have really powerful and secret ones. I agree wholeheartedly that mysteries and plots laced in secret is an integral part of WoD and vampire.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Strange... When coincidence seems too convenient, I prefer to call it fate.</i><br /> <br /> <b><sup>-Blood Omen: Legacy of Kain</sup> </b>d=","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25534-banu_saulot","name":"Banu_Saulot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25534_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}